        {
          "path": "_m/w/9/z/h/calle-real-iloilo-wp/museum-of-economic-history-for.jpg",
          "filename": "Museum of Economic History formerly Elizalde and Co. Building along JM Basa St. in Iloilo City.jpg",
          "caption": "The Museum of Economic History formerly the Elizalde and Co. building along Calle Real stands as a proud testament to Iloilo’s rich mercantile heritage. Housed in a beautifully restored colonial-era building, the museum highlights the city’s pivotal role as a major trading hub in the Visayas during the Spanish and American periods.\nThrough its well-curated exhibits, the museum chronicles the growth of commerce and industry — from sugar and textiles to global trade connections that shaped Iloilo into the “Queen City of the South” before World War II. Artifacts, archival photographs, and interactive displays tell stories of enterprising Ilonggos and foreign merchants who contributed to the city’s economic rise.\nManaged by the National Museum of the Philippines, the Museum of Economic History not only preserves the grandeur of Iloilo’s past but also celebrates its continuing legacy as a center of culture, commerce, and innovation in the region.\n\nSent",
          "alt": "The Museum of Economic History formerly the Elizalde and Co. building along Calle Real stands as a proud testament to Iloilo’s rich mercantile heritage. Housed in a beautifully restored colonial-era building, the museum highlights the city’s pivotal role as a major trading hub in the Visayas during the Spanish and American periods.\nThrough its well-curated exhibits, the museum chronicles the growth of commerce and industry — from sugar and textiles to global trade connections that shaped Iloilo into the “Queen City of the South” before World War II. Artifacts, archival photographs, and interactive displays tell stories of enterprising Ilonggos and foreign merchants who contributed to the city’s economic rise.\nManaged by the National Museum of the Philippines, the Museum of Economic History not only preserves the grandeur of Iloilo’s past but also celebrates its continuing legacy as a center of culture, commerce, and innovation in the region.\n\nSent",
